      ":question/id": "7d5cf986-bb83-58fe-b183-abc83e7c7042",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{7}{- 8 x^{2} + 3 x + 12}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=7$ and $v(x)=- 8 x^{2} + 3 x + 12,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u^{\\prime}(x)&=0,\\\\v^{\\prime}(x)&=3 - 16 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{(0)\\cdot (- 8 x^{2} + 3 x + 12)-(7)\\cdot (3 - 16 x)}{(- 8 x^{2} + 3 x + 12)^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{112 x - 21}{\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 3 x + 12\\right)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
