      ":question/id": "67ce3755-e734-505a-8aa6-7b80238da618",
      ":question/problem": "Find the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{x - 1}{k + x^{2} + 12 x}.$ Treat $k$ as a constant.\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x - 1$ and $v(x)=k + x^{2} + 12 x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u^{\\prime}(x)&=1,\\\\v^{\\prime}(x)&=2 x + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{(1)\\cdot (k + x^{2} + 12 x)-(x - 1)\\cdot (2 x + 12)}{(k + x^{2} + 12 x)^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{k - x^{2} + 2 x + 12}{\\left(k + x^{2} + 12 x\\right)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{k - x^{2} + 2 x + 12}{\\left(k + x^{2} + 12 x\\right)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
